    "question": {
      ":question/id": "8bbd6dc9-f1b8-56d8-89c1-1f43be484423",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
      ":question/problem": "For the parametric curve \n\n$$\nx=2 \\sin {2 t},\\,y=3 \\cos^{2} {t},\\,0\\le t<2\\pi,\n$$\n\ncalculate the slope of the tangent to the curve at the point where $t=\\frac{\\pi}{6}.$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{y'(t)}{x'(t)}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $x$ and $y,$ we get\n\n$$\nx'(t)=4 \\cos {2 t},\\,y'(t)=- 6 \\sin {t} \\cos {t}=- 3 \\sin {2 t}.\n$$\n\n \n\nSo we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =\\frac{\\left(- 3 \\sin {2 t}\\right)}{\\left(4 \\cos {2 t}\\right)} \\\\  & =- \\frac{3 \\tan {2 t}}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, when $t=\\frac{\\pi}{6},$ the slope of the tangent to the curve is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =- \\frac{3 \\tan {2 \\left(\\frac{\\pi}{6}\\right)}}{4} \\\\  & =- \\frac{3 \\left(\\sqrt{3}\\right)}{4} \\\\  & =- \\frac{3 \\sqrt{3}}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
